Since you're looking at the NVIDIA Clara ecosystem, the gold standard for a "basic" project is a 3D Segmentation pipeline using Dictionary Transforms. This is how real-world medical imaging data (CT/MRI) is actually handled.
This project uses the Spleen 3D Dataset and showcases how MONAI simplifies 3D volume processing.
🏥 3D Spleen Segmentation Project
python
import torch
from monai.networks.nets import UNet
from monai.transforms import (
    Compose, LoadImaged, EnsureChannelFirstd, 
    Spacingd, Orientationd, ScaleIntensityRanged, Resized
)
from monai.data import Dataset, DataLoader

# 1. Dictionary-based Data Prep
# In MONAI, "d" at the end of a transform means it expects a Python Dictionary
data_dicts = [{"image": "img1.nii.gz", "label": "seg1.nii.gz"}] # Placeholder paths

transforms = Compose([
    LoadImaged(keys=["image", "label"]),
    EnsureChannelFirstd(keys=["image", "label"]),
    # Standardize orientation to RAS (Right, Anterior, Superior)
    Orientationd(keys=["image", "label"], axcodes="RAS"),
    # Rescale CT intensity (Hounsfield Units) for the Spleen
    ScaleIntensityRanged(
        keys=["image"], a_min=-57, a_max=164,
        b_min=0.0, b_max=1.0, clip=True,
    ),
    Resized(keys=["image", "label"], spatial_size=(128, 128, 128))
])

# 2. Optimized Dataset
# Use CacheDataset in real runs to store results in RAM
ds = Dataset(data=data_dicts, transform=transforms)
loader = DataLoader(ds, batch_size=1)

# 3. NVIDIA-Optimized 3D UNet
device = torch.device("cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu")
model = UNet(
    spatial_dims=3,
    in_channels=1,
    out_channels=2, # Spleen vs Background
    channels=(16, 32, 64, 128, 256),
    strides=(2, 2, 2, 2),
    num_res_units=2,
).to(device)

print("Project Setup Complete: Model & Pipeline ready for training.")
Use code with caution.

🛠️ Why this is "NVIDIA Clara" Style:
Medical Domain Specifics: Notice ScaleIntensityRanged. In medical imaging, you don't just normalize 0-255; you target specific Hounsfield Units (HU) for the organ you want to see.
Spatial Awareness: Orientationd ensures that if one scan was taken head-first and another feet-first, the AI sees them both the same way.
Memory Management: 3D scans are huge. Using spatial_size=(128, 128, 128) or Sliding Window Inference (included in MONAI) prevents GPU "Out of Memory" errors.
To run this properly, you can download the sample data from the Medical Segmentation Decathlon.
